The files under src/main/resources/RegionData/ are derived from that project's
region data (format version 0.8). Redistribution of those files retains the
copyright notice above as the BSD 2-Clause License requires.

They are no longer byte-identical to upstream, in two ways.

  1. The six citizens in 14131.json were named after the Barrows Brothers, with
     matching examine text, and are now generic "Barrow wight"s; their
     placements, models and animations are unchanged, only the identities.

  2. 12598.json (the Grand Exchange) gained six records that are not theirs at
     all: the "friend cameo" figures, authored for this project and switched off
     by default. They are identifiable in the data by "cameo": true, and they
     are the only records using the "npcAppearanceId" field, which is also this
     project's addition to the format. Nothing upstream authored was touched.

BSD-2 permits both modifications — this note exists so nobody assumes the
vendored data still matches the source it came from.

The animation name -> id table in
src/main/java/com/matthewmariner/livelycities/LivelyAnimation.java is likewise
derived from that project (its AnimationID enum), because the region data
addresses animations by those exact names — the names are part of the data
format, not an implementation choice. One constant in it is not theirs:
HumanLeanReady (916, gameval AnimationID.HUMAN_LEAN_READY), added for the cameo
records above because their table carries no human lean.

The model lighting parameters in LivelyEntity (ambient 64, contrast 850,
x -30, y -50, z -30, passed to Model#light) are also taken from that project.
Unlike the vertex scale/translate convention around them — which follows
RuneLite's own 128-units-per-tile geometry and is not theirs to own — those five
values are a look, chosen by its authors, and reproducing a look is derivation.

Apart from the three items above, the Java source in this repository is an
independent implementation and is not derived from theirs.

